.TH CGREP 1 "2024-01-15" "Gab n' Go" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cgrep \- search through LLM context window sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cgrep
.RB [ \-l ]
.RB [ \-L ]
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-H ]
.RB [ \-c ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-o ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RB [ \-E ]
.RB [ \-F ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-B
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-A
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-C
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-include
.IR glob ]
.RB [ \-\-exclude
.IR glob ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR format ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.I pattern
.IR paths ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cgrep
(context grep) searches through conversation content stored in
LLM agent session files using PCRE regular expressions.
.PP
The tool reads actual session data from each agent's storage
format and applies pattern matching to find matches within
conversation messages.
.PP
Output follows grep conventions. Every match line is prefixed
with the session it came from
.RI ( agent/session_id : lineno : text ),
context lines use a
.RB \(dq - \(dq
separator in the same position
.RI ( agent/session_id - lineno - text ),
and non-contiguous groups are separated by
.BR \-\- .
The
.B \-h
and
.B \-H
options suppress and restore the session prefix.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-files\-with\-matches
List only session IDs that contain matches, not the matches themselves.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-files\-without\-match
List only session IDs that do NOT contain matches.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-no\-filename
Suppress the session path prefix. Match lines become
.IR lineno : text .
With
.B \-c
only the counts are printed.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-with\-filename
Force the session path prefix on. This is the default; if
.B \-h
and
.B \-H
are both given,
.B \-H
wins.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-count
Print only a count of matching lines per session.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress all output; report the result through the exit status only.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-max\-count
.I N
Stop after
.I N
matches in each session.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-only\-matching
Print only the matched text, one hit per line, instead of whole lines.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-word\-regexp
Select only matches that are surrounded by word boundaries.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-line\-regexp
Select only matches that span the entire line.
.TP
.BR \-E ", " \-\-extended\-regexp
Use extended (default) regular expressions. Accepted for grep
compatibility; this is already how cgrep interprets patterns.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-fixed\-strings
Treat the pattern as a fixed string instead of a regular expression.
.TP
.BR \-\-include ", " \-\-exclude
.I glob
Only search (or skip) sessions whose
.I agent/session_id
path matches the glob. Repeatable; both may be combined, where a session
must match an
.B \-\-include
and must not match any
.BR \-\-exclude .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-invert\-match
Select non-matching lines.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-ignore\-case
Ignore case distinctions in patterns and input.
.TP
.BR \-B ", " \-\-before
.I N
Print
.I N
lines of context before each match.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-after
.I N
Print
.I N
lines of context after each match.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-context
.I N
Print
.I N
lines of context before and after each match.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format
.I format
Set output format. Available formats:
.RS
.TP
.B default
Human-readable with line numbers.
.TP
.B json
JSON array of match objects.
.TP
.B xml
XML document with session and match elements.
.TP
.B md
Markdown with match highlights.
.RE
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version information and exit.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I pattern
PCRE regular expression pattern to search for.
.TP
.I paths
One or more agent/session paths in the format:
.RS
.TP
.BI agent /
Search all sessions for agent.
.TP
.BI agent/session_id
Search a specific session.
.RE
.PP
Multiple paths can be specified separated by spaces.
.SH EXAMPLES
Search for "error" in all opencode sessions:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cgrep "error" "opencode/*"
opencode/ses_abc123:3:user: I'm getting an error in my code
opencode/ses_abc123:7:assistant: The error is caused by...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Drop the session prefix for a single-session search:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cgrep -h "error" "opencode/ses_abc123"
3:user: I'm getting an error in my code
7:assistant: The error is caused by...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Search case-insensitively and show context:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cgrep -h -i -C 2 "error" "opencode/*"
1-assistant: Hello! How can I help?
2-user: I'm getting an error in my code
3-user: I'm getting an ERROR in my code
4-assistant: Let me help you debug this
5-assistant: I see the issue now
.fi
.RE
.PP
List sessions containing "TODO":
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cgrep -l "TODO" "opencode/*" "claude-code/*"
opencode/ses_abc123
claude-code/ses_xyz789
.fi
.RE
.PP
Count matches per session:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cgrep -c "import" "opencode/*"
opencode/ses_abc123:5
opencode/ses_def456:2
.fi
.RE
.PP
Invert match (find lines WITHOUT "test"):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cgrep -v "test" "opencode/ses_abc123"
1:user: Hello world
4:assistant: Here is some code
.fi
.RE
.PP
Output as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cgrep --format json "error" "opencode/*"
[
  {
    "session": "opencode/ses_abc123",
    "line_num": 3,
    "line": "user: I'm getting an error"
  }
]
.fi
.RE
.PP
Output as XML:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cgrep --format xml "error" "opencode/*"
<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<matches>
  <session id="ses_abc123" agent="opencode">
    <match line="3">
      <message>user: I'm getting an error</message>
    </match>
  </session>
</matches>
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
One or more matches found.
.TP
.B 1
No matches found.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred (invalid pattern, unknown agent, missing session data).
An error takes precedence over a match.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cdir (1)
.SH AUTHORS
Gab n' Go project.
